#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:69
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Feature work must be linked to a GitHub issue. Scan before creating, and never create one without the BA's permission — an issue is the stakeholder's record, not the agent's scratchpad."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:64
#. Diagram label
msgid "Feature?"
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:34
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Holds the one gate whose \"no\" branch ends the entire process rather than routing into a child — GW_Feature's own documentation: a subprocess would have to re-ask the same question to route on the answer. From there it carries the BA's need through all six phases as a straight chain of subprocess calls, with no branching of its own."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:73
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Identify the stakeholders, synthesise a needs statement from the sources, and loop until the BA and the stakeholders both recognise it. The loop is the phase: a needs statement nobody confirmed is an agent's summary, not a requirement."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:68
#. Diagram label
msgid "Link the work to an issue"
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:77
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Map the current workflow, then define the requirements and the impact they carry, and loop until approved. Same shape as Phase 1 and for the same reason: an unapproved requirement is a proposal."
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:52
#. Diagram label
msgid "Need identified"
msgstr ""

#: processes/process/crdm-requirements.bpmn:114
#. Diagram label
